# ---------------- L5 ----------------
("05_gpcr","### What a GPCR is",["7 passes; N-terminus outside, C-terminus inside","Ligand binds outside, G protein binds the inside loops","One ligand can hit different GPCRs in different tissues with opposite effects (epinephrine: β raises cAMP, α2 lowers it)"],"Over 700 GPCRs in humans and the list of ligand types: know they exist, don't memorize the list."),
("05_gpcr","### The trimeric G protein",["Gα holds GDP (off) or GTP (on); Gβγ is a stuck-together pair","The receptor is a GEF: it makes Gα drop GDP so GTP can jump in","GTP-bound Gα and Gβγ split, and EITHER can activate an effector","Gα turns itself off by hydrolyzing GTP (built-in timer)"],"21 Gα / 6 Gβ / 12 Gγ counts: recognize, low priority."),
("05_gpcr","#### The main kinds of Gα",["Gαs raises cAMP, Gαi lowers it, Gαq activates PLC (IP3 + DAG)","Gαt (transducin) is the odd one: it LOWERS cGMP via PDE","Three effector families: adenylyl cyclase, PLC, ion channels"],"Every example receptor in the table: learn β-adrenergic, α2, muscarinic, odorant and rhodopsin; skim the rest."),
("05_gpcr","### Branch 1: adenylyl cyclase",["Adenylyl cyclase: ATP → cAMP (+ pyrophosphate)","PDE destroys cAMP → 5'-AMP (the eraser)","Gαs = gas pedal, Gαi = brake on the same cyclase; the cell sums them"],None),
("05_gpcr","#### Same cAMP, different jobs",["Same cAMP, different response, because PKA finds different targets in each cell"],"The full tissue table: know heart (faster), liver/muscle (glycogen breakdown), kidney (water). Skim the rest."),
("05_gpcr","#### Drugs that copy or block",["Agonist = mimics and turns ON (often tighter, lower Kd)","Antagonist = sits in the site and BLOCKS","Isoproterenol: agonist, opens airways (asthma). Alprenolol: beta-blocker antagonist, slows heart"],None),
("05_gpcr","#### Protein kinase A",["cAMP binds the R subunits, which release active C subunits","Binding is cooperative (2nd cAMP binds more easily) → sharp switch","PKA is a Ser/Thr kinase"],None),
("05_gpcr","#### Glycogen:",["PKA turns glycogen synthase OFF and phosphorylase kinase ON","Phosphorylation can inhibit; always check which enzyme","Chain: PKA → phosphorylase kinase → phosphorylase → glycogen breakdown"],None),
("05_gpcr","#### Signal amplification",["Each step activates many of the next, so 1 hormone → 10^8 glucose-1-phosphate"],"Exact numbers at each step: know start (1) and end (10^8), skim the middle."),
("05_gpcr","#### cAMP can also turn on genes",["PKA C enters the nucleus, phosphorylates CREB","CREB + CBP bind the CRE and turn on genes (slow effect)"],None),
("05_gpcr","### Turning the cAMP signal off",["Three off switches: hormone lets go, Gα hydrolyzes GTP, PDE destroys cAMP","Cholera toxin ADP-ribosylates Gαs → can't hydrolyze GTP → stuck ON → diarrhea","'Can't hydrolyze GTP' or 'can't be down regulated' = signal stays on"],None),
("05_gpcr","### Branch 2: phospholipase C",["Gαq → PLC-β (the effector enzyme) cuts PIP2","IP3 (water-soluble) → opens ER Ca2+ channels","DAG (lipid) stays in membrane → with Ca2+ activates PKC","PIP2 comes from PI → PI4P → PIP2 (two kinases)"],"Fura-2 dye details: just know it shows Ca2+ rising."),
("05_gpcr","#### What calcium does",["Ca2+ works mostly through calmodulin (4 Ca2+ sites)","Ca2+/CaM activates CaM-kinase II, myosin light chain kinase, and PDE (so Ca2+ can lower cAMP)","CaM-kinase II stays 50-80% active after Ca2+ leaves (memory)"],"Table 15-3 tissue list: skim."),
("05_gpcr","#### Turning the Ca2+ signal off",["Pumps (to ER and outside), Na+/Ca2+ exchanger, binding proteins","IP3 → IP2 by a phosphatase closes the ER channels","Cytosol ~10^-7 M vs outside ~10^-3 M"],None),
("05_gpcr","#### Gated and non-gated channels",["Non-gated: always open (resting K+ channel)","Gated by voltage, ligand (outside or inside), or stretch"],None),
("05_gpcr","#### Electrochemical gradients",["Net push = concentration gradient + membrane potential","Resting potential about -70 mV, near the K+ equilibrium potential","K+ high inside, Na+ high outside; Na+/K+ ATPase builds the K+ gradient","Channels never pump: Na+ in = depolarize, K+ out = hyperpolarize"],"The 59 mV beaker numbers: know the idea (voltage moves toward the permeable ion), not the exact value."),
("05_gpcr","#### G proteins that open K+ channels",["Heart: ACh → muscarinic GPCR (Gi) → Gβγ opens K+ channels","K+ out → hyperpolarize → slower heartbeat","The subunit is Gβγ, NOT Gα"],None),
("05_gpcr","### Vision:",["Rhodopsin = opsin + 11-cis-retinal; light → all-trans","Transducin → PDE → cGMP DOWN → channels CLOSE → rod HYPERpolarizes","Dark: channels open, -30 mV, constant transmitter release","Vitamin A makes retinal; deficiency = night blindness"],"Amplification numbers (500 transducins, 250 channels): skim."),
("05_gpcr","### Smell",["Odorant → OR → Gαolf → AC3 → cAMP → CNG channel opens","Ca2+ then opens a Cl- channel and Cl- LEAVES (more depolarization)","Smell raises cAMP to open; vision lowers cGMP to close"],"1000 mouse / 350 human receptor counts: low priority."),
("05_gpcr","### Nitric oxide",["ACh → Ca2+/CaM → NO synthase makes NO from arginine (endothelium)","NO diffuses into smooth muscle → guanylyl cyclase → cGMP → relax"],None),
